  "adds": "Each record says what one address of one type held at power on, and each says in as many words that the printed range beside it is evidence about a page rather than about the unit. The page says what a parameter's default is, in the parameter's own units, and does not say which byte that is. Neither says the two agree, and turning one into the other needs a third table -- the conversion grid, for every parameter whose byte does not carry its setting. This file is where the three are joined.",
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "reading": "What the records call a default is not what the unit powers up holding, but whatever the run before them left at that address.",
      "candidate": null,
      "standing": false,
      "equivalent_under_this_test": false,
      "separated_by": {
        "observable": "Whether one address of one type returns the same byte every time.",
        "predicts": {
          "read at power on": "the same byte for one address of one type, always",
          "left behind": "a byte that depends on what ran before"
        },
        "sensitivity_here": "The records were written over several sessions, and a type whose parameters share addresses with another type returns each type's own value at them.",
        "margin": null
      },
      "ruled_out_by": "It would have to be wrong the same way at every one of these parameters, and wrong in the direction of a page nothing in those runs had read. The records were written over several sessions and a type whose parameters share addresses with another type returns each type's own value at them, which is not something a residue of the previous write does."
    }
  ],
  "refuted_by": "One parameter powering up on a byte its page prints no default at, where the page marks a default and the conversion pins it to a single byte. Those are where this claim is at risk; the rest sit inside a run of bytes the page prints one setting across, and could have agreed by landing anywhere in it.",
  "could_have_been_refuted_by": "The comparison ran over every parameter of every type the list prints and the records hold, not over a chosen few. It was written to report disagreements and it reported four on its first run -- all four its own, from taking the first byte printing a setting where the page prints that setting at several.",
  "what_it_would_change": "The held-tone survey writes one type and nothing else, so every other parameter of that type is at its default and the readings are of a type in a state the archive could describe only as `whatever the unit powers up holding`. It can now be named, parameter by parameter, in the units the parameter is set in -- which is what an excursion measured off that take has to be interpreted against.",
  "what_this_does_not_close": [
    {
      "what": "The parameters the page marks no readable default for.",
      "why": "Most are printed as a list of states or as a run between two ends, with no third part to be the default: the printing marks those in boldface and nothing that reads the page as laid-out text can see it. The rest have ends this could not read as numbers.",
      "what_would_close_it": "Reading the boldface, which needs the page read as marked-up text rather than as laid-out text. The defaults themselves are already in the records; what is missing is the page's own statement of them."
    }
  ]
}
